Subtract corresponding entries in the two <matrices>:
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}2&-10&15\\-6&-3&-6\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Both matrices have shape $2\times3$, so this subtraction is defined.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Using row-column <matrix multiplication>, the second row of the first <matrix> is $(1,6)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}3&7\\1&6\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}2&4\\0&0\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}6&12\\2&4\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The row-column product is the <dot product> $4\cdot6+8\cdot3+1\cdot0$, giving $\boxed{48}$. Its shape is $1\times1$.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The first <matrix product> gives the column $(37,37,37)^T$. Its product with the final row is an <outer product>, not a scalar:
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}37\\37\\37\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}-2&0&2\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}-74&0&74\\-74&0&74\\-74&0&74\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Row reduction, or the <cofactor> formula for a <matrix inverse>, gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\1&2&3\\1&4&9\end{pmatrix}^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}3&-5/2&1/2\\-3&4&-1\\1&-3/2&1/2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplying by the right-hand-side column $(5,13,35)^T$ gives, at $\lambda=3$,
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(0,2,3).}
$$
For general $\lambda$, the <coefficient matrix> has <determinant> $(\lambda-1)(\lambda-2)$. Thus other parameter values give a unique solution. If $\lambda=1$, subtracting the first equation from the second gives $y=8$, while subtracting it from the third gives $3y=30$, a contradiction. If $\lambda=2$, subtraction gives $y+z=8$ and $3y+3z=30$, again inconsistent. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{no solution exactly when }\lambda=1\text{ or }2.}
$$

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Write $r=|\mathbf r|$ and $q=\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf r$. Away from the origin, $\nabla q=\mathbf p$ and $\nabla r^{-m}=-m\mathbf r/r^{m+2}$. The <product rule> gives
$$
\nabla\cdot(q\mathbf r r^{-5})=q r^{-5}+q(3r^{-5}-5r^{-5})=-q r^{-5},\qquad \nabla\cdot(\mathbf p r^{-3})=-3q r^{-5}.
$$
Consequently the <divergence> of $\mathbf E=3q\mathbf r r^{-5}-\mathbf p r^{-3}$ is zero. For any closed surface bounding a region that excludes the origin, the field is smooth throughout that region, and the <divergence theorem> proves
$$
\boxed{\int_A\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf S=0.}
$$
For the infinite cylinder, use $\mathbf r=(a\cos\phi,a\sin\phi,z)$ and outward <unit normal> $(\cos\phi,\sin\phi,0)$. With $\mathbf p=(0,0,1)$, $q=z$, $dS=a\,d\phi\,dz$, and $\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n=3az/(a^2+z^2)^{5/2}$. Hence the direct <surface flux> is
$$
\int_0^{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^\infty\frac{3a^2z}{(a^2+z^2)^{5/2}}dz\,d\phi=\boxed0.
$$
The integrand is <odd> in $z$ and absolutely integrable. This calculation treats the unbounded cylindrical side directly, without applying the bounded-region <divergence theorem> across the field's singularity.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

For an $N+1$ times continuously differentiable <function>, <Taylor's theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sum_{n=0}^N\frac{f^{(n)}(x_0)}{n!}(x-x_0)^n+\frac{f^{(N+1)}(\xi)}{(N+1)!}(x-x_0)^{N+1},}
$$
where $\xi$ lies between $x_0$ and $x$. An infinite <Taylor series> represents the function when its remainder tends to zero; this holds locally for an <analytic function>, rather than for every smooth function automatically.

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Integrate the <geometric series> for $1/(1+x)$ from zero to $x$. The first three nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{\ln(1+x)=x-\frac{x^2}2+\frac{x^3}3+O(x^4).}
$$
The resulting <Taylor series> converges for $|x|<1$.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For fixed real $a\ne0$, write the square root as $|a|\sqrt{1+x^2/a^2}$ and use the <binomial series> with exponent $-1/2$:
$$
\boxed{\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+a^2}}=\frac x{|a|}-\frac{x^3}{2|a|^3}+\frac{3x^5}{8|a|^5}+O(x^7).}
$$
The <absolute values> are necessary for negative $a$. This <Taylor series> is valid for $|x|<|a|$. If $a=0$, the quotient is $x/|x|$ for $x\ne0$; it has no continuous extension at zero and therefore **has no Taylor expansion about zero**.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Factor the <Gaussian function> as $e^{-(x-a)^2}=e^{-a^2}e^{2ax}e^{-x^2}$. Multiplying the two <exponential series> gives
$$
e^{-(x-a)^2}=e^{-a^2}\left[1+2ax+(2a^2-1)x^2+\left(\frac43a^3-2a\right)x^3+O(x^4)\right].
$$
For $a\ne0,\pm1/\sqrt2$, its first three nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{e^{-a^2}\bigl[1+2ax+(2a^2-1)x^2\bigr].}
$$
Vanishing coefficients require separate treatment in this <Taylor expansion of a translated Gaussian>. If $a=0$, the first three nonzero terms are $\boxed{1-x^2+x^4/2}$. If $a=\pm1/\sqrt2$, they are
$$
\boxed{e^{-1/2}\left(1+2ax-\frac{4a}3x^3\right).}
$$
The latter expression retains the cubic term because the quadratic coefficient is zero.

= d
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use the real <logarithm> near zero and subtract its two <Taylor series>:
$$
\ln\frac{1-x}{1+2x^2}=\ln(1-x)-\ln(1+2x^2)=\left(-x-\frac{x^2}2-\frac{x^3}3\right)-2x^2+O(x^4).
$$
Thus the first three nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{-x-\frac52x^2-\frac13x^3.}
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <Einstein summation convention>, with repeated component indices summed. The following three expressions are listed separately below. For cyclic invariance of the <trace>,
$$
\operatorname{tr}(ABC)=a_{ij}b_{jk}c_{ki}=c_{ki}a_{ij}b_{jk}=\operatorname{tr}(CAB),
$$
where the middle equality commutes scalar entries, not the <matrices> themselves. If $A$ is invertible, this gives
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}(ABA^{-1})=\operatorname{tr}(BA^{-1}A)=\operatorname{tr}B.}
$$
This is <similarity invariance of trace>.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Taking the diagonal sum of the <matrix product> gives
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}(AB)=a_{ij}b_{ji}.}
$$
Both repeated indices are summed from one to $n$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply row-column <matrix multiplication> twice:
$$
\boxed{(ABC)_{ij}=a_{ik}b_{k\ell}c_{\ell j}.}
$$
The indices $i,j$ are free, while $k,\ell$ are summed.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <identity matrix> has entries given by the <Kronecker delta>, so
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}I=\delta_{ii}=n.}
$$
Each of the $n$ diagonal entries is one.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Expand $(I+B)^2$ in components:
$$
(A^2)_{ij}=(\delta_{ik}+b_{ik})(\delta_{kj}+b_{kj})=\boxed{\delta_{ij}+2b_{ij}+b_{ik}b_{kj}}.
$$
If $B^2=0$, all powers $B^k$ for $k\geq2$ vanish. The matrix <binomial theorem>, valid here because $I$ commutes with $B$, therefore yields
$$
\boxed{A^n=I+nB,\qquad(A^n)_{ij}=\delta_{ij}+nb_{ij}.}
$$
These <powers of the identity plus a square-zero matrix> can also be proved by induction: $(I+nB)(I+B)=I+(n+1)B$. For the displayed example, $B^2=0$ directly, and
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}1&p\\0&1\end{pmatrix}^{n}=\begin{pmatrix}1&np\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
This is the repeated <function composition> of a <shear mapping>. The argument gives every nonnegative integer $n$; since $(I+B)^{-1}=I-B$, the same formula also holds for negative integers.

