= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply the numerator and denominator by the <complex conjugate> $1+i$: $(1+i)/(1-i)=(1+i)^2/2=i$, whose square is $-1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=-1,\qquad\operatorname{Im}=0.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The number $ie^{i\phi}$ has <modulus> one and <argument> $\phi+\pi/2$ modulo $2\pi$. Therefore its <complex logarithm> has values
$$
\boxed{\ln(ie^{i\phi})=i(\phi+\pi/2+2\pi k),\quad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Every value has <real part> zero. For the <principal complex logarithm>, choose the unique integer giving imaginary part in $(-\pi,\pi]$. The unwrapped value $\phi+\pi/2$ is the principal imaginary part only when it already lies in that interval.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In <polar form>, a cube root of one has <modulus> one and <argument> $2\pi k/3$. The three <roots of unity> are
$$
\boxed{1,\qquad\frac{-1+i\sqrt3}2,\qquad\frac{-1-i\sqrt3}2.}
$$
They are equally spaced on the <unit circle>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $-8=8e^{i(\pi+2\pi k)}$. Its cube roots have <modulus> two and <arguments> $(\pi+2\pi k)/3$, for $k=0,1,2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{1+i\sqrt3,\qquad -2,\qquad 1-i\sqrt3.}
$$
Each cube equals $-8$, and a cubic <polynomial> has no further distinct roots.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $b\ne0$, write $z=bw$. The equation becomes $w^2+w+1=0$, whose roots are $(-1\pm i\sqrt3)/2$, both of <modulus> one. Hence $|z|=|b|$. If $b=0$, the double root is $z=0$, giving the same relation. Therefore
$$
\boxed{|z|\leq1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad -1\leq b\leq1.}
$$

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <probability density function> $P$, the <mean> and <variance> are
$$
\boxed{\mu=\int_{-\infty}^\infty xP(x)dx,\qquad \sigma^2=\int_{-\infty}^\infty(x-\mu)^2P(x)dx=\mathbb E[X^2]-\mu^2.}
$$
These formulas assume the required <moments> are finite; finiteness of the second moment ensures a finite mean as well.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The triangular <probability density function> is normalized because $2\int_0^1(1-x)dx=1$. It is <even>, so the mean integrand is <odd> and $\mu=0$. Its <variance> is consequently its second <moment>:
$$
\boxed{\mu=0,\qquad\sigma^2=2\int_0^1x^2(1-x)dx=2\left(\frac13-\frac14\right)=\frac16.}
$$

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The standard <normal density> is <even>, so $x^3P(x)$ is <odd>. Since <Gaussian decay dominates every power>, the integrand is absolutely integrable. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X^3]=\int_{-\infty}^\infty x^3P(x)dx=0.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the standard <normal density>, $P'(x)=-xP(x)$. Using <integration by parts>,
$$
\mathbb E[X^4]=-\int_{-\infty}^\infty x^3P'(x)dx=-[x^3P(x)]_{-\infty}^\infty+3\int_{-\infty}^\infty x^2P(x)dx.
$$
The boundary term vanishes because <Gaussian decay dominates every power>. Since the given <mean> is zero and <variance> is one, $\mathbb E[X^2]=1$, yielding the <Gaussian fourth moment>
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X^4]=3.}
$$
